It complements the earlier episode `2026-08-18-19-computational-possibility-formation-currentness-and-repair.md`.


### Hostile cases

The audit tested:

- pointwise versus uniform quantification;
- adversarial alternation;
- randomized probability scope;
- private/public randomness visibility;
- interactive roles;
- family quantification;
- discoverability versus existence.


## Transition 10 — Runtime / Interlocus applicability dogfood

A live route/profile outage plus a successful local Runtime witness created a three-way applicability split:

- narrow constructive actual witness -> bounded `APPLIES`;
- exact capability premise unavailable/currentness drift -> no current `APPLIES`, older basis may be `EXPIRED`;
- broad system-wide impossibility without exhaustive candidate coverage -> `UNRESOLVED`.

Surviving practical laws:

- `HistoricalCapability != CurrentCapability`;
- `ExactRouteUnavailable != GlobalNetworkImpossibility`;
- `OneRouteFailure != ActualCandidateCoverage`.

This was direct real-owner evidence for the constructive/impossibility directional asymmetry in Applicability.


## Process-level lessons

- project formation does not imply the best explanatory model has already been found;
- a thin primitive core can support a very rich relational theory;
- hostile tests should target quantifier/dependency/composition/transport structure, not just primitive nouns;
- temporary audit lenses should be deleted or demoted before canonicalization;
- consumer dogfood can justify an independent research owner's value without forcing that owner to absorb downstream engineering truth;
- a real scoped failure is often evidence for a narrower claim, not a global impossibility theorem;
- positive reconstruction should follow destructive audit before expanding engineering materialization.

